    <summaries>
      <summary>
        <versionCode>00</versionCode>
        <actionDate>2026-09-24</actionDate>
        <actionDesc>Introduced in House</actionDesc>
        <updateDate>2026-10-06T14:19:57Z</updateDate>
        <text><![CDATA[<p><strong>Motion Picture, Television, and Entertainment Revitalization Act</strong></p><p>This bill establishes a federal business tax credit for a percentage of the compensation paid for services performed in the United States related to a qualified film or television production. (Conditions and exceptions apply.)</p><p>The bill defines a <em>qualified film or television production</em> as any production that is</p><ul><li>a feature film, television pilot, or television season;</li><li>produced in the ordinary course of business for commercial purposes;</li><li>capitalized;</li><li>completed during the tax year with more than $1 million in total costs; and</li><li>filmed in the United States for 75% of the photography days.</li></ul><p>Under the bill, various types of programming are not a qualified film or television production, including news programs, live sporting events, and programming intended primarily for social media platforms.</p><p>The amount of the tax credit is 20% (base tax credit percentage) of the compensation paid for services performed in the United States related to a qualified film or television production. The base tax credit percentage increases by increments of 5% (up to a maximum tax credit percentage of 30%) if</p><ul><li>at least 30% of the production is filmed in a federally declared disaster area or rural qualified opportunity zone,</li><li>the production is independently produced, or</li><li>the production meets certain multi-state or increased domestic production requirements.</li></ul><p>Special rules apply to animations, visual effects productions, and post-production activities.</p><p>Finally, the tax credit may be transferred to an unrelated third party in exchange for cash.</p>]]></text>
      </summary>
    </summaries>
